<h2>India's best-diversified export line</h2>
<p>Dehydrated onion stands out in our research as one of the most geographically spread Indian agricultural exports we've verified — roughly 120 destination markets sharing USD 221.4 million in calendar 2024 trade, with no single market anywhere close to dominant. The United States leads at USD 25.7 million, but Brazil follows closely at USD 25.2 million, with Germany, Indonesia, Belgium, the UK, Russia, the Netherlands, Spain and Canada rounding out a genuinely global spread.</p>

<h2>Brazil's position specifically</h2>
<p>Brazil's USD 25.2 million places it as India's second-largest single market for this product, reflecting demand from Brazil's substantial food-processing and food-manufacturing sector, where dehydrated onion's practicality — long shelf life, no cold chain, consistent specification — fits industrial production needs better than sourcing fresh onion seasonally.</p>

<h2>Why dehydrated sidesteps fresh onion's biggest complication</h2>
<p>This is worth understanding if you've looked at Indian onion sourcing before and been put off by policy volatility. Fresh onion, classified under HS 0703.10, has been subject to a Minimum Export Price and, at various points, an export duty, plus a full six-month export ban in 2023-24 — all measures India uses to manage domestic onion supply and consumer prices. Dehydrated onion falls under a completely different HS heading (0712.20) and hasn't faced that same restriction history, making it a meaningfully more predictable product to build a recurring sourcing programme around.</p>

<h2>Form and specification</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Form</strong> — kibbled, chopped, minced, granules or powder</li>
  <li><strong>Mesh size</strong> — for granules and powder, affecting flow and dispersion</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture</strong> — typically ≤5-6%</li>
  <li><strong>Colour</strong> — white or red onion base</li>
  <li><strong>Microbiological panel</strong> — standard plate count, yeast/mould, salmonella and E. coli limits, particularly relevant for formulated food manufacturing use</li>
</ul>

<h2>Logistics</h2>
<p>Standard dry container shipping — no reefer required — with an 18-24 month shelf life. This is meaningfully simpler logistics than fresh onion or any fresh produce line, which is part of why food manufacturers gravitate toward the dehydrated form even where fresh onion would technically serve the same culinary purpose.</p>

<h2>What Brazilian buyers typically ask about first</h2>
<p>In our experience, Brazilian food-manufacturing buyers focus early conversations on three things: consistent particle-size distribution within a mesh grade (important for automated dosing equipment in production lines), microbiological testing documentation they can pass through to their own quality systems, and confirmation of packaging that survives Brazil's climate and import handling without moisture ingress. None of these are unusual requirements, but having the documentation ready before it's requested tends to move a first order along faster than waiting to be asked.</p>
